\n    40→    \"source\": \"Hacker News\",\n    41→    \"url\": \"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=40865432\",\n    42→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-17\",\n    43→    \"topics\": [\n    44→      \"android\",\n    45→      \"google\",\n    46→      \"ai-agents\",\n    47→      \"mobile-development\",\n    48→      \"cli\",\n    49→      \"hacker-news\"\n    50→    ],\n    51→    \"type\": \"discussion\"\n    52→  },\n    53→  {\n    54→    \"title\": \"The future of everything is lies, I guess: Where do we go from here?\",\n    55→    \"description\": \"Kyle Kingsbury (Aphyr) discusses the implications of AI-generated content on truth and trust. Explores the concept of 'meat shields' - humans accountable for AI systems - and the broader societal impact of AI-generated falsehoods.\",\n    56→    \"author\": \"Kyle Kingsbury (Aphyr)\",\n    57→    \"source\": \"Hacker News\",\n    58→    \"url\": \"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=40863210\",\n    59→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-17\",\n    60→    \"topics\": [\n    61→      \"ai-ethics\",\n    62→      \"truth\",\n    63→      \"accountability\",\n    64→      \"ai-safety\",\n    65→      \"society\",\n    66→      \"hacker-news\"\n    67→    ],\n    68→    \"type\": \"discussion\"\n    69→  },\n    70→  {\n    71→    \"title\": \"Claude Opus 4.7 release discussion on Hacker News\",\n    72→    \"description\": \"Hacker News discussion about Anthropic's Claude Opus 4.7 release. Users discuss capabilities, pricing, performance improvements, and comparisons with other models like GPT-5.4 and open-source alternatives.\",\n    73→    \"author\": \"Hacker News Community\",\n    74→    \"source\": \"Hacker News\",\n    75→    \"url\": \"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=40871234\",\n    76→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-17\",\n    77→    \"topics\": [\n    78→      \"anthropic\",\n    79→      \"claude\",\n    80→      \"opus\",\n    81→      \"llm\",\n    82→      \"model-release\",\n    83→      \"hacker-news\"\n    84→    ],\n    85→    \"type\": \"discussion\"\n    86→  },\n    87→  {\n    88→    \"title\": \"Qwen3.6-35B-A3B on my laptop drew me a better pelican than Claude Opus 4.7\",\n    89→    \"description\": \"Simon Willison compared two major model releases from April 16, 2026: Qwen3.6-35B-A3B from Alibaba and Claude Opus 4.7 from Anthropic, using his 'pelican riding a bicycle' benchmark. The local Qwen model produced better results than the premium Claude Opus model, highlighting the growing capabilities of open/local models.\",\n    90→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    91→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    92→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/16/qwen-opus-pelican/\",\n    93→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-16\",\n    94→    \"topics\": [\n    95→      \"LLM\",\n    96→      \"local-llms\",\n    97→      \"model-comparison\",\n    98→      \"anthropic\",\n    99→      \"qwen\",\n   100→      \"generative-ai\"\n   101→    ],\n   102→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   103→  },\n   104→  {\n   105→    \"title\": \"Release: llm-anthropic 0.25 with Claude Opus 4.7 support\",\n   106→    \"description\": \"New release of llm-anthropic plugin adding support for Claude Opus 4.7 with thinking_effort: xhigh parameter, new thinking_display and thinking_adaptive boolean options, and increased default max_tokens to maximum allowed for each model.\",\n   107→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   108→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   109→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/16/llm-anthropic-025/\",\n   110→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-16\",\n   111→    \"topics\": [\n   112→      \"LLM\",\n   113→      \"anthropic\",\n   114→      \"claude\",\n   115→      \"developer-tools\",\n   116→      \"python\"\n   117→    ],\n   118→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   119→  },\n   120→  {\n   121→    \"title\": \"datasette.io news preview tool built with Claude Artifacts\",\n   122→    \"description\": \"Simon used Claude Artifacts to build a custom preview UI for datasette.io news section, taking advantage of Claude's ability to clone GitHub repos and examine content. Demonstrates vibe coding with Claude for building developer tools.\",\n   123→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   124→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   125→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/16/datasette-io-preview/\",\n   126→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-16\",\n   127→    \"topics\": [\n   128→      \"vibe-coding\",\n   129→      \"claude\",\n   130→      \"tools\",\n   131→      \"datasette\",\n   132→      \"developer-tools\"\n   133→    ],\n   134→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   135→  },\n   136→  {\n   137→    \"title\": \"TREX: Automating LLM Fine-tuning via Agent-Driven Tree-based Exploration\",\n   138→    \"description\": \"Research paper introducing TREX, a system that automates LLM fine-tuning using agent-driven tree-based exploration. Addresses the challenge of automating the fine-tuning process for large language models through intelligent exploration strategies.\",\n   139→    \"author\": \"Zerun Ma, Guoqiang Wang, Xinchen Xie, et al.\",\n   140→    \"source\": \"ArXiv\",\n   141→    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.14116\",\n   142→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-16\",\n   143→    \"topics\": [\n   144→      \"llm\",\n   145→      \"fine-tuning\",\n   146→      \"agents\",\n   147→      \"automation\",\n   148→      \"reinforcement-learning\",\n   149→      \"arxiv\"\n   150→    ],\n   151→    \"type\": \"paper\"\n   152→  },\n   153→  {\n   154→    \"title\": \"Memory Transfer Learning: How Memories are Transferred Across Domains in Coding Agents\",\n   155→    \"description\": \"Research paper investigating how coding agents transfer memory across different domains. Explores memory mechanisms that enable AI coding agents to apply learned knowledge from one domain to solve problems in another domain.\",\n   156→    \"author\": \"Kangsan Kim, Minki Kang, Taeil Kim, et al.\",\n   157→    \"source\": \"ArXiv\",\n   158→    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.14004\",\n   159→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-16\",\n   160→    \"topics\": [\n   161→      \"coding-agents\",\n   162→      \"memory-transfer\",\n   163→      \"machine-learning\",\n   164→      \"ai\",\n   165→      \"arxiv\"\n   166→    ],\n   167→    \"type\": \"paper\"\n   168→  },\n   169→  {\n   170→    \"title\": \"WebXSkill: Skill Learning for Autonomous Web Agents\",\n   171→    \"description\": \"Paper presenting WebXSkill, a framework for learning skills that autonomous web agents can use. Addresses the challenge of enabling web agents to acquire and reuse skills for autonomous web navigation and interaction tasks.\",\n   172→    \"author\": \"Zhaoyang Wang, Qianhui Wu, Xuchao Zhang, et al.\",\n   173→    \"source\": \"ArXiv\",\n   174→    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.13318\",\n   175→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-16\",\n   176→    \"topics\": [\n   177→      \"web-agents\",\n   178→      \"skill-learning\",\n   179→      \"autonomous-agents\",\n   180→      \"ai\",\n   181→      \"arxiv\"\n   182→    ],\n   183→    \"type\": \"paper\"\n   184→  },\n   185→  {\n   186→    \"title\": \"From Feelings to Metrics: Understanding and Formalizing How Users Vibe-Test LLMs\",\n   187→    \"description\": \"Research paper studying how users perform informal 'vibe testing' of LLMs and formalizing this process into measurable metrics. Addresses the gap between user intuition and formal evaluation methods for language models.\",\n   188→    \"author\": \"Itay Itzhak, Eliya Habba, Gabriel Stanovsky, Yonatan Belinkov\",\n   189→    \"source\": \"ArXiv\",\n   190→    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.14137\",\n   191→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-16\",\n   192→    \"topics\": [\n   193→      \"llm-evaluation\",\n   194→      \"vibe-testing\",\n   195→      \"user-studies\",\n   196→      \"metrics\",\n   197→      \"ai\",\n   198→      \"arxiv\"\n   199→    ],\n   200→    \"type\": \"paper\"\n   201→  },\n   202→  {\n   203→    \"title\": \"Gemini 3.1 Flash TTS with prompt-directed voice generation\",\n   204→    \"description\": \"Google released Gemini 3.1 Flash TTS, a text-to-speech model that can be directed using detailed prompts. Simon experimented with directing accents and characteristics, finding the prompting approach surprisingly verbose but effective at generating different regional accents.\",\n   205→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   206→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   207→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/15/gemini-flash-tts/\",\n   208→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-15\",\n   209→    \"topics\": [\n   210→      \"google\",\n   211→      \"text-to-speech\",\n   212→      \"tools\",\n   213→      \"ai\",\n   214→      \"prompt-engineering\",\n   215→      \"gemini\",\n   216→      \"llm-release\"\n   217→    ],\n   218→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   219→  },\n   220→  {\n   221→    \"title\": \"Datasette 1.0a27 replaces CSRF tokens with Sec-Fetch-Site headers\",\n   222→    \"description\": \"Major Datasette update replacing Django-style CSRF form tokens with modern browser headers (Sec-Fetch-Site) based on Filippo Valsorda's research. Also introduces RenameTableEvent for table rename operations and new actor parameter for client methods.\",\n   223→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   224→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   225→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/15/datasette-1.0a27/\",\n   226→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-15\",\n   227→    \"topics\": [\n   228→      \"datasette\",\n   229→      \"security\",\n   230→      \"csrf\",\n   231→      \"python\",\n   232→      \"web-development\"\n   233→    ],\n   234→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   235→  },\n   236→  {\n   237→    \"title\": \"OpenAI announces GPT-5.4-Cyber model and Trusted Access program\",\n   238→    \"description\": \"OpenAI released GPT-5.4-Cyber, a variant fine-tuned for defensive cybersecurity use cases, extending their Trusted Access for Cyber verification program. Appears to be a response to Anthropic's Claude Mythos and Project Glasswing.\",\n   239→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   240→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   241→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/14/openai-cyber/\",\n   242→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-14\",\n   243→    \"topics\": [\n   244→      \"security\",\n   245→      \"ai\",\n   246→      \"openai\",\n   247→      \"anthropic\",\n   248→      \"ai-security-research\",\n   249→      \"llms\"\n   250→    ],\n   251→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   252→  },\n   253→  {\n   254→    \"title\": \"Cybersecurity Looks Like Proof of Work Now\",\n   255→    \"description\": \"UK's AI Safety Institute evaluation of Claude Mythos shows that more tokens spent equals better security results. Drew Breunig notes this creates an economic incentive: to harden a system you need to spend more tokens discovering exploits than attackers will spend exploiting them. Makes open source libraries more valuable as security investment can be shared.\",\n   256→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison (citing Drew Breunig)\",\n   257→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   258→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/14/cybersecurity-proof-of-work/\",\n   259→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-14\",\n   260→    \"topics\": [\n   261→      \"open-source\",\n   262→      \"ai\",\n   263→      \"security\",\n   264→      \"llms\",\n   265→      \"vibe-coding\",\n   266→      \"ai-security-research\"\n   267→    ],\n   268→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   269→  },\n   270→  {\n   271→    \"title\": \"Claude Code redesign focuses on managing multiple AI agents\",\n   272→    \"description\": \"Anthropic redesigned Claude Code desktop app with focus on managing multiple AI agents simultaneously. New features include sidebar for session management, drag-and-drop layout customization, built-in terminal, and file editor.\",\n   273→    \"author\": \"Emma Roth, The Verge\",\n   274→    \"source\": \"The Verge\",\n   275→    \"url\": \"https://www.theverge.com/2026/4/14/claude-code-redesign-multiple-ai-agents\",\n   276→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-14\",\n   277→    \"topics\": [\n   278→      \"anthropic\",\n   279→      \"claude\",\n   280→      \"ai-agents\",\n   281→      \"developer-tools\",\n   282→      \"ui-design\",\n   283→      \"the-verge\"\n   284→    ],\n   285→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   286→  },\n   287→  {\n   288→    \"title\": \"Gemini with Personal Intelligence rolling out globally\",\n   289→    \"description\": \"Google's Gemini Personal Intelligence feature, which pulls information from Gmail, Photos, Search, and YouTube history, is rolling out globally except UK, Switzerland, and EEA. Initially available for paid subscribers before free users.\",\n   290→    \"author\": \"Emma Roth, The Verge\",\n   291→    \"source\": \"The Verge\",\n   292→    \"url\": \"https://www.theverge.com/2026/4/14/gemini-personal-intelligence-global-rollout\",\n   293→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-14\",\n   294→    \"topics\": [\n   295→      \"google\",\n   296→      \"gemini\",\n   297→      \"personal-ai\",\n   298→      \"privacy\",\n   299→      \"feature-rollout\",\n   300→      \"the-verge\"\n   301→    ],\n   302→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   303→  },\n   304→  {\n   305→    \"title\": \"Chrome now lets you turn AI prompts into repeatable 'Skills'\",\n   306→    \"description\": \"Google added AI Skills feature to Chrome, allowing users to save AI prompts as reusable workflows. Skills can be shared and used across different contexts, representing a shift toward prompt-based automation.\",\n   307→    \"author\": \"Tom Warren, The Verge\",\n   308→    \"source\": \"The Verge\",\n   309→    \"url\": \"https://www.theverge.com/2026/4/14/chrome-ai-skills-prompts-workflows\",\n   310→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-14\",\n   311→    \"topics\": [\n   312→      \"google\",\n   313→      \"chrome\",\n   314→      \"ai-prompts\",\n   315→      \"automation\",\n   316→      \"workflow\",\n   317→      \"the-verge\"\n   318→    ],\n   319→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   320→  },\n   321→  {\n   322→    \"title\": \"Steve Yegge vs Google on AI adoption rates\",\n   323→    \"description\": \"Heated exchange between Steve Yegge (claiming Google has low AI adoption similar to John Deere) and Google engineers Addy Osmani and Demis Hassabis (disputing the claims, stating 40K SWEs use agentic coding weekly). Highlights industry-wide AI adoption patterns: 20% agentic power users, 20% refusers, 60% using chat tools.\",\n   324→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   325→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   326→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/13/google-ai-adoption/\",\n   327→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-13\",\n   328→    \"topics\": [\n   329→      \"google\",\n   330→      \"agentic-engineering\",\n   331→      \"ai\",\n   332→      \"llms\",\n   333→      \"engineering-culture\"\n   334→    ],\n   335→    \"type\": \"discussion\"\n   336→  },\n   337→  {\n   338→    \"title\": \"Exploring the new servo embeddable browser engine crate\",\n   339→    \"description\": \"Simon investigated the new servo v0.1.0 crate, an embeddable browser engine for Rust. Built a CLI tool 'servo-shot' for taking screenshots using servo. Found that compiling Servo to WebAssembly isn't feasible due to heavy thread usage, but built a playground for html5ever/markup5ever WebAssembly builds.\",\n   340→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   341→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   342→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/13/servo-crate/\",\n   343→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-13\",\n   344→    \"topics\": [\n   345→      \"research\",\n   346→      \"browsers\",\n   347→      \"rust\",\n   348→      \"webassembly\",\n   349→      \"claude-code\",\n   350→      \"servo\"\n   351→    ],\n   352→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   353→  },\n   354→  {\n   355→    \"title\": \"Bryan Cantrill on LLMs and the virtue of laziness\",\n   356→    \"description\": \"Bryan Cantrill argues that LLMs lack the virtue of laziness - work costs nothing to an LLM, so they don't optimize for future time by creating crisp abstractions. Human laziness forces us to develop good abstractions to avoid wasting our own time. LLMs left unchecked will make systems larger, not better.\",\n   357→    \"author\": \"Bryan Cantrill (cited by Simon Willison)\",\n   358→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   359→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/13/bryan-cantrill-laziness/\",\n   360→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-13\",\n   361→    \"topics\": [\n   362→      \"ai\",\n   363→      \"llms\",\n   364→      \"ai-assisted-programming\",\n   365→      \"generative-ai\",\n   366→      \"software-engineering\"\n   367→    ],\n   368→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   369→  },\n   370→  {\n   371→    \"title\": \"Audio transcription with Gemma 4 E2B and MLX\",\n   372→    \"description\": \"Simon shared a recipe for transcribing audio files on macOS using the 10.28 GB Gemma 4 E2B model with MLX and mlx-vlm using 'uv run'. Demonstrates local model usage for speech-to-text tasks with good results.\",\n   373→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   374→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   375→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/12/gemma-transcription/\",\n   376→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-12\",\n   377→    \"topics\": [\n   378→      \"uv\",\n   379→      \"mlx\",\n   380→      \"ai\",\n   381→      \"gemma\",\n   382→      \"llms\",\n   383→      \"speech-to-text\",\n   384→      \"python\",\n   385→      \"generative-ai\"\n   386→    ],\n   387→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   388→  },\n   389→  {\n   390→    \"title\": \"SQLite 3.53.0 with ALTER TABLE constraints and query formatting\",\n   391→    \"description\": \"SQLite 3.53.0 release adds ALTER TABLE support for adding/removing NOT NULL and CHECK constraints, new json_array_insert() function, and significant CLI improvements including a new Query Results Formatter library. Simon compiled the formatter to WebAssembly and built a playground interface.\",\n   392→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   393→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   394→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/11/sqlite-3.53.0/\",\n   395→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-11\",\n   396→    \"topics\": [\n   397→      \"sql\",\n   398→      \"sqlite\",\n   399→      \"webassembly\",\n   400→      \"tools\"\n   401→    ],\n   402→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   403→  },\n   404→  {\n   405→    \"title\": \"Andrej Karpathy on the growing AI capability gap\",\n   406→    \"description\": \"Andrej Karpathy tweeted about the growing gap in AI capability understanding based on access points and domains. Notes that OpenAI's free Advanced Voice Mode runs on older GPT-4o era models while their highest-tier Codex model can spend 1 hour coherently restructuring entire codebases. Highlights that domains with explicit reward functions (coding, security) are improving faster due to RL trainability and B2B value.\",\n   407→    \"author\": \"Andrej Karpathy (cited by Simon Willison)\",\n   408→    \"source\": \"Twitter\",\n   409→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/10/ai-capability-gap/\",\n   410→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-10\",\n   411→    \"topics\": [\n   412→      \"andrej-karpathy\",\n   413→      \"generative-ai\",\n   414→      \"openai\",\n   415→      \"chatgpt\",\n   416→      \"ai\",\n   417→      \"llms\",\n   418→      \"ai-capability\"\n   419→    ],\n   420→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   421→  }\n   422→]\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
      }
